---
const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const baseUrl = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : rawBase + '/';

const LIMITATIONS_URL =
  'https://github.com/Gluflex/iv-flow-monitor-v2/blob/v2/docs/limitations.md';

const items = [
  {
    section: '§17',
    title: 'Position-dependence — V_CAL_K does not generalise across mount heights.',
    impact: 'Architectural',
    body: 'On the afternoon of 2026-05-13 the device was mounted at three different heights on the same drip chamber (same set, same fluid, same board). The K required to match gravimetric ground truth varied from 0.28 to 1.27 across positions — a 4.5× spread driven by mount geometry alone. Three contaminants of the chord measurement were isolated: an umbilical at TOP when mounted close to the drip orifice, backsplash at BOT when mounted close to the pool, and a threshold-vs-peak mismatch that shifts with the boot-cal baseline. Several algorithm variants (BOT_core only, TOP_low only, adaptive EMA, hybrid splash-detection) were tried; no single (algorithm, K) combination passed ±5 % at all positions. Conclusion: with Rev-B optics, the optical chord-time architecture does not deliver a position-invariant V_CAL_K. The two beams do deliver position-agnostic velocity and reliable drop counting — what they don\'t deliver is a position-invariant chord. The Rev-C path is named: a second pair of horizontally-separated beams, wider TOP-to-BOT separation, and TOP/BOT gain equalisation.',
    anchor: '17-position-dependence--v_cal_k-does-not-generalise-across-mount-heights',
  },
  {
    section: '§1',
    title: 'Drop-shape oscillation is the fundamental residual error.',
    impact: 'Fundamental',
    body: 'The chord-time architecture measures the vertical extent of each drop at the beam plane and assumes a sphere to compute volume. Drops oscillate between oblate (squashed) and prolate (elongated) for the first 10–30 mm after detachment; the beam catches each drop at an unpredictable point in this oscillation. Over many drops the bias averages out (V_CAL_K = 1.27 works on the run mean), but the per-drop CV stays inflated at ±30 % across V_50_01..05. A Rev-C optical front-end with two horizontally-separated beam pairs catching horizontal as well as vertical extent would let the firmware infer the oscillation phase.',
    anchor: '1-drop-shape-oscillation-is-the-fundamental-residual-error',
  },
  {
    section: '§2',
    title: 'Two-orifice divergence invalidates per-drop matching.',
    impact: 'Methodological',
    body: 'The device measures drops at the drip-chamber orifice; the scale captures mass at the end-of-tubing orifice. These two orifices have different geometry and surface-tension dynamics, so they drip at different rates with different drop sizes. The 2026-05-13 campaign saw end-orifice drop counts 1.5–2× the chamber-drop counts. Mass conservation still gives a clean shared quantity (total mass passed), so the valid ground truth at the per-drop level is V_true_chamber = mass / N_UART, not a per-drop pairing. tools/match_scale_drops.py is preserved as an exploratory diagnostic only.',
    anchor: '2-two-orifice-divergence-invalidates-per-drop-matching',
  },
  {
    section: '§3',
    title: 'TOP-vs-BOT optical asymmetry (backsplash + intrinsic).',
    impact: 'Optical',
    body: 'The TOP and BOT photodiodes report systematically different pulse widths for the same drop. The 2026-05-13 backsplash falsifiability test repositioned the device higher on the chamber; the BOT/TOP ratio fell from 1.75–2.50× to ~1.13×, suggesting the BOT photodiode sees a real splash-driven extension that the threshold-margin clips. A Rev-C optical front-end with the BOT beam moved further from the pool, plus TOP/BOT gain equalisation, would close the gap.',
    anchor: '3-top-vs-bot-optical-asymmetry-backsplash--intrinsic',
  },
  {
    section: '§5',
    title: 'V_CAL_K is fitted to the runs it is validated against.',
    impact: 'Methodological',
    body: 'The scalar correction V_CAL_K = 1.27 is the unweighted mean of per-run k_pre across V_50_01..04. Reporting per-run residual against these four runs is therefore a self-consistency check, not an independent validation. V_50_05 (run after baking V_CAL_K into firmware) is the first generalisation check. Multi-day / multi-board / multi-fluid generalisation is out of Rev-B scope.',
    anchor: '5-v_cal_k--127-is-fitted-to-the-same-runs-it-is-validated-against',
  },
  {
    section: '§7',
    title: 'Tethered 3.3 V supply.',
    impact: 'Scope',
    body: 'The validation dataset is collected with 3.3 V supplied directly to MCU VDD via ST-Link, bypassing the TPS610981 boost converter from the single-cell battery design. The dataset validates the optical detection chain, sphere-model calibration, and dual-beam architecture. The field power chain (1 × AA Li-ion → boost → MCU) is deferred to Rev-C.',
    anchor: '7-tethered-33-v-supply',
  },
];
---
<section id="limitations">
  <div class="eyebrow">Honest weaknesses</div>
  <h2>What Rev-B does not resolve.</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    Six load-bearing limitations of the Rev-B validation, in order of impact on
    the conclusions. The full list of 17 is in
    <a href={LIMITATIONS_URL} rel="noopener"><code>docs/limitations.md</code></a>.
    Each item below links to its full section. The position-dependence finding
    (§17) is the single most important Rev-C-relevant learning of the bench
    campaign.
  </p>

  <ol class="lim-list">
    {items.map((it) => (
      <li class="lim-item">
        <a class="lim-row" href={`${LIMITATIONS_URL}#${it.anchor}`} rel="noopener">
          <span class="lim-section">{it.section}</span>
          <div class="lim-text">
            <div class="lim-head">
              <h3>{it.title}</h3>
              <span class={`lim-impact lim-impact-${it.impact.toLowerCase()}`}>{it.impact}</span>
            </div>
            <p>{it.body}</p>
          </div>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <div class="position-eyebrow">§17 — the raw evidence.</div>
  <p class="position-lead">
    Three views of the same 2026-05-14 PM raw-waveform capture set
    (TOP + BOT photodiode signals, ADC counts above baseline, time
    relative to the TOP-entry trigger). The capture firmware
    (<code>ENABLE_RAW_CAPTURE</code> on the
    <code>feat/raw-capture-rev-c</code> branch) is <em>not</em> the Rev-B
    submission firmware; submission firmware = HEAD of <code>v2</code>.
  </p>

  <figure class="position-figure">
    <img src={`${baseUrl}analysis/raw_combined_P3_minus4mm.png`}
         alt="TOP and BOT beam-occlusion waveforms overlaid in a single panel for 23 consecutive drops at mount position P3 (4 mm below canonical). TOP curves (blue) peak around +2 ms after the trigger anchor with a small secondary shoulder approaching from negative time. BOT curves (orange) peak around +11 ms with a bifurcated structure showing a second peak at +14 ms." />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>One drop, both beams, one chart.</strong> 23 drops at
      <code>P3 (-4 mm)</code>, TOP (blue) and BOT (orange) overlaid in
      the same panel. The ~11 ms transit gap between TOP firing and BOT
      firing is the dual-beam architecture's velocity signal — directly
      visible. The two position-dependent contaminants of the chord
      measurement are also direct-readable: <em>(i)</em> the TOP shoulder
      approaching the trigger around <code>−2 ms</code> is the
      <strong>umbilical</strong> — the fluid filament still tethering the
      drop to the chamber tip; <em>(ii)</em> the bifurcated BOT peak
      around <code>+11 ms / +14 ms</code> is the <strong>backsplash</strong>
      trailing edge — particles crossing BOT after the drop body has
      cleared.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="position-figure">
    <img src={`${baseUrl}analysis/raw_median_bands_all_positions.png`}
         alt="Median trace and 25 to 75 percent IQR band for each of three mount positions, TOP and BOT beams shown in separate panels. TOP medians overlap closely across all three positions. BOT medians shift systematically in timing — P1 canonical mount peaks first, P3 minus 4 mm peaks last with a visible second hump." />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>What position-dependence actually looks like.</strong>
      Median trace (line) + 25–75 % IQR band (shaded) per mount position,
      both beams. TOP shapes are largely <strong>position-invariant</strong>
      — the medians overlap closely across P1, P2, P3.
      BOT timing shifts <strong>systematically</strong> with mount
      geometry: P1 canonical peaks ~10 ms after trigger, P3 (-4 mm) peaks
      ~12 ms after trigger with a visible second hump. The §17 finding
      is that this BOT-shape variation is exactly what defeats a
      position-invariant chord-time → volume estimate. Bench n_drops:
      P1=40, P2=22, P3=23.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="position-figure">
    <img src={`${baseUrl}analysis/raw_overlays_P1_high.png`}
         alt="Two-panel overlay of 40 drops at the canonical V_50_05-era mount position. Left panel TOP beam peaks consistently around 225 to 260 ADC counts. Right panel BOT beam peaks 397 to 508 ADC counts with a small late-time hump visible after most drops." />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>40 drops at the canonical mount</strong> — the densest
      single-mount capture in the campaign. Per-channel peak ranges are
      tight (TOP 225–260, BOT 397–508; ±7 % and ±12 % around the median),
      meaning the device's per-drop response is repeatable; what makes
      <code>V_CAL_K = 1.27</code> hold at <em>this</em> mount within
      ±30 % is the same data driving the
      <a href="#analysis">predicted accuracy envelope</a> a panel up.
      Move the device 4 mm down the chamber (P3 above) and the BOT-shape
      family changes — that's what §17 is about.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<style>
  #limitations {
    padding-top: 56px;
    padding-bottom: 56px;
  }
  #limitations h2 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 40px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    max-width: 22ch;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }
  .lim-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 36px 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .lim-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 64px 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
    padding: 20px 22px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ir);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    transition: background-color 120ms ease, border-color 120ms ease;
  }
  .lim-row:hover {
    background: var(--bg-3);
    border-left-color: var(--flow);
  }
  .lim-section {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--ir);
    padding-top: 2px;
  }
  .lim-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .lim-head h3 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .lim-impact {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 10.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    padding: 2px 7px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .lim-impact-architectural { color: var(--ir); border-color: var(--ir); }
  .lim-impact-fundamental   { color: var(--ir); border-color: var(--ir); }
  .lim-impact-optical       { color: var(--flow); border-color: var(--flow); }
  .lim-text p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .position-eyebrow {
    margin: 48px 0 8px;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    color: var(--ir);
  }
  .position-lead {
    margin: 0 0 24px;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .position-lead code,
  .position-lead em {
    font-style: normal;
  }
  .position-lead code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    background: var(--bg-3);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .position-lead em { color: var(--ir); font-weight: 600; }

  .position-figure {
    margin: 0 0 28px;
    background: #ffffff;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding: 18px;
  }
  .position-figure:last-of-type {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .position-figure img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .position-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 14px;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .position-figure figcaption strong {
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .position-figure figcaption em {
    color: var(--ir);
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .position-figure figcaption code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    background: var(--bg-3);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .lim-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 6px; }
    .lim-section { font-size: 13px; }
    #limitations h2 { font-size: 30px; }
  }
</style>
